S=['COC directly states counterclockwise skating and a lane change every lap. This supports the keyed direction; the alternatives are competing course hypotheses.', 'COC states the direction, distance equalization and outside-lane priority when both athletes arrive together. The explanation preserves those conditions.', 'The direction and lane exchange are source-supported. The note remains factually true despite the editorial problem with its link to the clockwise option.', 'The source lists 500, 1000 and 1500 m and retains the same counterclockwise route and per-lap lane exchange. The note distinguishes varying distance from fixed direction.', 'COC grants outside-lane right-of-way only on simultaneous arrival at the crossover. The note keeps that condition.', 'The source says athletes change lanes each lap to equalize distance while skating counterclockwise. The note accurately distinguishes a lane change from a reversal.', 'ISU section 7 defines a block as one configuration with at least three lines. The other formations have different basic requirements. A wheel may have a three-line parallel variant, but three lines are not its minimum requirement.', 'The explanation correctly maps block lines, circle skaters, the maximum two lines of a Line, and skaters per wheel spoke. Pages 43–44 and their diagrams confirm the units.', 'ISU states a Circle has at least three skaters. This is a participant count, not a line count.', 'ISU explicitly limits the Line formation to a maximum of two lines.', 'ISU requires at least two skaters per wheel spoke. Later shape variants do not remove that requirement.', 'ISU defines a closed block as outlined and filled with skaters, and an open block as only outlined. The note adds a classification beyond the three-line requirement.', 'IIHF explicitly links penalties and the opponent’s numerical advantage to power play, giving 5-on-4, 5-on-3 and 4-on-3 as common examples. The key is factually supported.', 'IIHF supports the penalty-induced deficit, the other team’s power play and these common combinations. The explanation is an accurate summary.', 'IIHF describes three twenty-minute stop-time periods, with overtime and a shootout if required. This supports the overtime note.', 'The complete Icing entry gives the basic untouched-puck definition and resulting stoppage, followed by exceptions. The note is a brief definition, not an exhaustive statement that overrides those exceptions.', 'The Plus/Minus entry defines an individual player statistic and excludes team goals scored during power play. The current note explicitly refers to players on the ice.', 'The Delayed Call entry says play continues with non-offending possession and stops when the offending team controls the puck. The note preserves the trigger.', 'IIHF defines an Empty-Net Goal as a goal against a team that pulled its goalkeeper. The Pulling entry adds an extra skater, the trailing team, late-game use and the unguarded goal.', 'Both entries support the definition and the usual late-game context. The source also describes delayed-penalty use; the explanation’s usual-case wording does not exclude that case.', 'IIHF explains penalty-based numerical advantage separately from pulling the goalkeeper. The note distinguishes these concepts without denying that scoring categories can overlap.', 'IIHF describes penalty-induced missing players and common one- or two-player deficits. The note describes that situation rather than asserting an absolute maximum.', 'The Game entry directly supplies regular time, overtime and conditional shootout context.', 'The Pulling entry expressly mentions the trailing team, closing minutes, extra skater and unguarded goal. The note uses a qualified “may”, not an exclusive rule.', 'FIS defines the release binding as releasing the boot at preset loads. It separately defines the interface and brake. The current function uniquely matches the binding.', 'The FIS definitions distinguish release bindings, the interface and the retention device. The explanation retains the racing-load qualification and correct component roles.', 'FIS states that the mounting method must not compromise the binding’s release mechanism. The note adds no unsupported torque or mounting dimensions.', 'FIS defines the interface as the functional component between the ski and release binding.', 'FIS states that running-surface edges transmit lateral forces and are mainly hard, wear-resistant material. The ski note accurately summarizes this.', 'FIS defines the brake as slowing a ski that came off after binding release. The note correctly describes a function after separation.']
E=['Easy: a familiar track-direction fact. The choices are directional hypotheses; confusing a crossover with a reversal is a plausible misconception. Opposed directions remain viable without knowing the rule, so absurd choices do not isolate the key.', 'Clear Russian and useful context about distance equalization and crossover priority. No ambiguity in the keyed direction.', 'FIX: the clockwise option’s note repeats the correct counterclockwise direction and adds a generic lane-exchange rule. That extra fact is not about the clockwise option. It needs a useful source-backed fact linked to its own variant, or an authorized alternative replacement.', 'The note addresses the distance-dependent hypothesis with actual distance variation and an invariant course. It explains which property varies rather than merely negating the key.', 'The correct option’s note adds conditional crossover priority beyond the asked direction. Overlap with the explanation is allowed.', 'The note addresses the crossover misconception by explaining what changes and why: lanes change to equalize distance, not direction.', 'Medium: understanding the named formations and their different minimum requirements needs topic knowledge. All four are genuine ISU formations and plausible parallel choices.', 'The explanation preserves the counted units. It does not claim that a wheel can never have three parallel lines.', 'The Circle note gives a useful minimum participant count specific to that formation.', 'The Line note gives a useful maximum specific to that formation.', 'The Wheel note gives a useful minimum per spoke specific to that formation.', 'The correct Block note adds the closed/open distinction beyond the three-line fact in the prompt and key.', 'HOLD medium: ordinary comparison of 5>4, 5>3 or 4>3 identifies “Большинство” without hockey knowledge. Overtime, icing and delayed whistle do not describe a numerical majority. This is the concrete GAME_SPEC:139 defect; no reclassification or exclusion is requested.', 'The factual explanation is clear, but it does not cure the prompt’s medium-level shortcut.', 'The overtime note supplies useful regular-versus-extra-time context for its own option.', 'The icing note gives a useful basic stoppage rule for its own option. A short note need not reproduce every exception.', 'The correct power-play note adds a statistical exclusion beyond player counts. The Russian is cumbersome, but “для игроков на льду” identifies the individual metric sufficiently.', 'The delayed-whistle note gives a specific possession-based stopping condition for its own option.', 'Easy: a familiar empty-goal situation with real scoring-category alternatives. Categories can overlap in a match, but only the empty-net description necessarily follows from the given goalkeeper withdrawal.', 'The explanation qualifies the usual late-game tactic and does not imply that goalkeeper withdrawal has only one possible use.', 'The power-play note explains penalty-based advantage and distinguishes it from withdrawal of a goalkeeper. This is useful own-option information.', 'The short-handed note supplies useful penalty-deficit and defensive context for its own option.', 'The overtime note supplies independent time-format context for its own option.', 'The correct empty-net note adds the trailing team and closing minutes, both absent from the prompt and key. The implied unguarded goal alone would not be enough, but those two details meet the additive rule.', 'Easy: a familiar binding function. The interface, ski and brake are real adjacent components, with the brake a plausible release-related distractor. The broad ski option does not isolate the key among the remaining devices.', 'The explanation clearly distinguishes the component that releases the boot from the interface and the brake.', 'The correct binding note adds a mounting requirement affecting release performance beyond the asked release function.', 'The interface note gives a useful placement fact for that component.', 'The ski note gives its own edges, forces and material fact, an allowed useful standalone fact for a wrong option.', 'The brake note clarifies its own function after release, distinct from connecting or releasing the boot.']
